ns  macroeconomics goes  stimulus can't work but you sort of  watch what's happened over the past few  months and it seems to be working a  little bit i mean  obviously like with cars for clunkers  all these people are rushing out to buy  the cars now that's going to  result at some point when it expires  there'll be a drop off in car  sales but if in the meantime  you've sort of gotten money flowing  through the economy again you've gotten  car makers starting to tool up for new  models you've gotten people  starting to spend on other things then  maybe it works so  there's not really any deeply  sophisticated economic theory behind all  of this  stimulus stuff it's basically that  world war ii the us government spent  just vastly more money than it took in  it was the most dramatic economic  stimulus  ever as far as i know and it worked and  that's basically all that  economists can say well it seemed to  work in world war ii so let's try it  again at a slightly smaller scale  um looking at your son i i have a  question that relates to him so when  he's in his  college economics classes what do you  think the dominant theories of the day  will be  that will drive those studies  i i mean as it stands now i bet they'll  be  pretty similar to the ones we've got now  um  the one area and there are people who've  been talking this up for  20 years is that their  insights coming mostly out of physics  but also other sciences about  complexity and evolutionary dynamics and  non-linear equations  that somehow could lead to a better  understanding of how  economies work and i'm i'm all for that  it's just it hasn't happened yet as far  as i know  although i got this long comment on my  blog the other day from a  professor in p king who says i was  totally unfair in short changing the  work being done  in evolutionary dynamics so maybe  there'll be more of that but i'm betting  a lot of these really basic economic  theories  i mean they're the stuff that's taught  in econ 1